function dive(item) {
  const path = RESEARCH + '/cmake-' + item.group + '/' + item.slug + '.md'
  const kindNote = item.kind === 'measure'
    ? 'This is a MEASUREMENT dive: the deliverable rests on scratch projects you build and run on the real CMake binaries named above, on every version the brief names. Quote the exact command and the exact observed output for each claim; a docs citation alone does not settle a claim in this dive.'
    : item.kind === 'exemplar'
      ? 'This is an EXEMPLAR dive: the deliverable rests on counts over the exemplar corpus, every count with the command that produced it and a per-repo table where it varies. Watch the pipefail + grep -q SIGPIPE trap that zeroed cells in wave 1: never pipe into grep -q under set -o pipefail.'
      : 'This is a WEB dive: the deliverable rests on primary sources you fetch. Where a claim is cheap to measure on the local CMake binaries, measure it anyway and say so.'
  return agent(
    `Model rationale: sonnet — research, counting or measurement and source digestion; volume work whose cost is in pages read and commands run, not in judgment.

${CONTEXT}

YOUR SUBAREA: ${item.label} (group: ${item.group_label}; rule family: ${item.id_family}; kind: ${item.kind})
${kindNote}

RESEARCH BRIEF (from the topic map; follow it exactly):
${item.brief}

${DIVE_CONTRACT(path)}

Return ONLY the structured receipt: the output path, source count, measurement count, your 8 strongest normative rules as one-liners, and up to 6 surprises.`,
    { label: 'dive:' + item.group + '/' + item.slug, phase: 'Dive', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'high', schema: DIVE_SCHEMA },
  )
}

function consolidate(group, items) {
  const path = RESEARCH + '/cmake-' + group + '.md'
  const dir = RESEARCH + '/cmake-' + group + '/'
  const families = [...new Set(items.map(i => i.id_family))]
  const family = families.join(' + ')
  const label = items[0].group_label
  const revise = EXISTING.has(group)
  const task = revise
    ? `You are REVISING an existing consolidated artifact to fold in a follow-up round it itself commissioned.

Read ${path} IN FULL, then every file in ${dir} in full (skip its scratch/ subdirectory except to confirm a measurement) — including the new artifact(s) this round produced: ${items.map(i => i.slug + '.md').join(', ')}.

Rewrite ${path} in place, preserving its structure:
- ID STABILITY IS A HARD CONTRACT. Every existing rule ID keeps its number and its meaning. Do not renumber, do not reorder into different IDs, do not reuse a retired number.
- New rules get NEW IDs continuing the sequence.
- If the follow-up CONTRADICTS an existing rule, change that rule's text in place and record the change — never leave both standing. A rule that overclaims a guarantee the new research shows does not exist is the most dangerous kind; fix it explicitly.
- Update the Verdict for whatever the follow-up settled, and REMOVE from Open questions whatever it answered. Where the research established a GAP rather than an answer, move it into the Verdict as a documented gap.
- Add or extend a "## Revision log": one line per change — what, which IDs, why.
- Update the frontmatter's consolidates list and add a revised: ${DATE} key.`
    : `You are consolidating a topic into the single authoritative artifact a later author works from.

Read EVERY file in ${dir} (list it first; read each in full; skip its scratch/ subdirectory except to confirm a measurement), plus the four audits under ${RESEARCH}/cmake-audit/ and the topic map's rows for this group. Where a file in that directory is a verification ledger (for example cps-verification.md), its verdicts override the dives it verified.

Write ${path}:
1. YAML frontmatter: title, topic, model: opus, id_family: ${families.join(', ')}, consolidates (the file list), date: ${DATE}.
2. "## Verdict" — 5-15 lines: the position this program takes, stated as DECISIONS, not as a survey. Where sub-researchers disagreed, decide and say why. Name the consumer kind each decision binds (CMake module author / library shipping a package / application consuming packages / recipe or port author / Bazel-wrapped dependency) when it differs.
3. "## The ruleset" — merged, de-duplicated, numbered rules, one subsection per ID family this group owns (${families.join(', ')}). Each entry: ID (<FAMILY>-nn), the rule as an imperative sentence, rationale (one line), verification (in the command shape above, or a named reading heuristic), severity (MUST / SHOULD / CONSIDER), and the CMake/Conan/vcpkg version floor it assumes. Drop rules that are generic model common knowledge and would not change behaviour; keep the ones an agent gets wrong without being told. Smallest set with the highest yield. Group rows by the check that catches them. A rule that rests only on argued or asserted evidence ships as CONSIDER, never MUST; a MUST needs normative or measured evidence.
4. "## Applied to find_ocx and the exemplars" — which rules find_ocx and the strict exemplars already satisfy, which prominent exemplars or find_ocx violate (repo@sha:path:line or file:line from the audits or sub-artifacts), and which are new commitments.
5. "## AI-agent failure modes" — merged, ranked by how often it bites, each with the mechanical check.
6. "## Open questions" — what needs an owner decision, and which subarea deserves ANOTHER research round (name the subarea and the question).
7. "## Sub-artifacts" — relative markdown links to each cmake-${group}/<file>.md with a one-line description.
8. "## Key sources" — the 10-15 best URLs or measurements across all sub-artifacts.

Merge rules: no claim without a citation that traces to a sub-artifact, a URL or a measurement; no duplicated rules; prefer the more specific formulation; when two sub-artifacts conflict, state the conflict and resolve it with a reason. A consolidation with no resolved conflict and no "violated" row is a survey wearing a verdict's clothes — do not produce one.`

  return agent(
    `Model rationale: opus — synthesis across conflicting sources plus normative decisions that later become enforced rules.

${CONTEXT}

TOPIC: ${label} (family ${family})

${task}

Do not modify any file other than ${path}.

Return ONLY the structured receipt: path, rule IDs with one-line imperatives, conflicts resolved, subareas needing another round, and the MUST count.`,
    { label: (revise ? 'revise:' : 'consolidate:') + group, phase: 'Consolidate', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', schema: CONSOLIDATE_SCHEMA },
  )
}

function verify(group) {
  const path = RESEARCH + '/cmake-' + group + '.md'
  const ledger = RESEARCH + '/cmake-' + group + '/verification-wave' + WAVE + '.md'
  return agent(
    `Model rationale: opus — adversarial verification of version floors and MUST rules before they become enforced configuration; the CPS round showed cheaper dives get about one version claim in seven wrong.

${CONTEXT}

You are the VERIFIER for ${path}. Try to REFUTE its load-bearing claims, then correct the consolidation in place.

1. Read ${path} in full. List every MUST rule and every claim of the form "<tool> <version> does/introduced/removed/deprecated X" (version floors in rule rows count).
2. For each, re-check it against the strongest evidence you can get cheaply: re-run it on the local CMake binaries (3.31, 4.3, 4.4) with a scratch project under ${MEASURE}/verify-${group}/ where a 20-line project settles it; otherwise re-fetch the primary source (CMake Help/*.rst at the relevant tag on gitlab.kitware.com raw; the tool's docs, changelog or source). Also run every MUST rule's verification command against a tiny planted violation and a tiny clean case where that is feasible, to prove the check can go red and stays green on clean input.
3. Classify each: CONFIRMED (evidence quoted), CORRECTED (what was wrong, what is true, evidence), or UNVERIFIABLE (what you tried). Default to UNVERIFIABLE, never CONFIRMED, when you could not check it.
4. Write the ledger to ${ledger} (YAML frontmatter: title, verifies, model: opus, date: ${DATE}; sections "## Ledger" as a table # | ID or claim | verdict | evidence | note, "## Verification commands exercised" with the planted-violation outcome per command, "## Unverifiable").
5. Edit ${path} in place for every CORRECTED item: change the rule text, floor, severity or verification; never renumber or delete an ID (retire it in place with a one-line reason if it must go); add a "## Revision log" line per change tagged "verify wave ${WAVE}"; add verified: ${DATE} to the frontmatter.

Modify only ${path}, ${ledger} and files under ${MEASURE}/verify-${group}/. Return the structured receipt.`,
    { label: 'verify:' + group, phase: 'Verify', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', schema: VERIFY_SCHEMA },
  )
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- Run
const groups = {}
for (const item of SELECTION) (groups[item.group] = groups[item.group] || []).push(item)
const entries = Object.entries(groups)
log('Wave ' + WAVE + ': ' + SELECTION.length + ' dives across ' + entries.length + ' groups (' + entries.map(([g, i]) => g + 'x' + i.length).join(', ') + '); revising: ' + (entries.filter(([g]) => EXISTING.has(g)).map(([g]) => g).join(', ') || 'none'))

const results = await pipeline(
  entries,
  ([group, items]) => parallel(items.map(item => () => dive(item).then(r => ({ slug: item.slug, ...(r || {}), ok: !!r })))),
  (dives, [group, items]) => {
    const ok = dives.filter(Boolean).filter(d => d.ok)
    if (!ok.length) { log('SKIP consolidate:' + group + ' — every dive dropped'); return { group, dives, consolidation: null } }
    if (ok.length < items.length) log('consolidate:' + group + ' proceeding with ' + ok.length + '/' + items.length + ' dives')
    return consolidate(group, items).then(c => ({ group, dives, consolidation: c }))
  },
  (r) => {
    if (!r || !r.consolidation) return r
    return verify(r.group).then(v => ({ ...r, verification: v }))
  },
)

const out = results.filter(Boolean)
const surprises = out.flatMap(r => (r.dives || []).filter(Boolean).flatMap(d => (d.surprises || []).map(s => r.group + '/' + d.slug + ': ' + s)))
const another = out.flatMap(r => ((r.consolidation && r.consolidation.needs_another_round) || []).map(s => r.group + ': ' + s))
const corrected = out.flatMap(r => ((r.verification && r.verification.corrected) || []).map(s => r.group + ': ' + s))
const musts = out.reduce((n, r) => n + ((r.consolidation && r.consolidation.must_count) || 0), 0)
log('Wave ' + WAVE + ' done: ' + out.filter(r => r.consolidation).length + '/' + entries.length + ' groups consolidated · ' + musts + ' MUST rules · ' + surprises.length + ' surprises · ' + another.length + ' follow-ups · ' + corrected.length + ' verifier corrections')
return { wave: WAVE, groups: out, surprises, needs_another_round: another, verifier_corrections: corrected, must_count: musts }
